m working through the twin prisoner's dilemma where each copy sees a different observation. Under A-FDT, intervening on my copy's function doesn't automatically fix the other copy's function, since its input differs. The open question is whether that other function's output tracks mine, and the inputs seem decision-irrelevant, so the reasoning may still be symmetric.\n\nI'm weighing the payoffs now: if the other copy tracks my output with credence rho, quiet beats defect when 2rho exceeds (1-rho)(1+s), which in the worst case of s=1 means rho must exceed 0.5. Given the symmetry, I think rho is above that threshold, so the argument passes.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8264,"completion_tokens":771,"total_tokens":9035,"cost":0.048476,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.048476,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.033056,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01542},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":407,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}